<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John III of Portugal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1502">1502</a>–<a href="/years/1557">1557</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as John the Pious, João III</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John III was King of Portugal from 1521 to 1557. To defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> against French traders, in 1534 he divided its coast into hereditary captaincies granted to nobles and officials, and when most of them failed he created a central government in 1549 under the governor-general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tome-de-sousa">Tomé de Sousa</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a>. His reign also saw the establishment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> in Portugal and the arrival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-iii-of-portugal.jpg" alt="John III of Portugal" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Cristóvão Lopes · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:D._Jo%C3%A3o_III_-_Crist%C3%B3v%C3%A3o_Lopes_(attrib).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
